                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f181eafd-e587-3759-8591-9e49f61c1ca3",
                                        "entry_id": "1c745381-18c3-3c7b-9ad8-dc2cddd3487a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The branch of mathematics dealing with those properties of a geometrical object (of arbitrary dimensionality) that are unchanged by continuous deformations (such as stretching, bending, etc., without tearing or gluing)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "20a81c4a-74f8-40f7-9c98-a0afb9c9dbd9",
                                                "sense_id": "f181eafd-e587-3759-8591-9e49f61c1ca3",
                                                "text": "1970 [Addison-Wesley], Stephen Willard, General Topology, 2012, Dover, page v,\nThis book is designed to develop the fundamental concepts of general topology which are the basic tools of working mathematicians in a variety of fields."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "715c3ecb-d8ae-44e6-b8d2-ca41cbbcc1b9",
                                                "sense_id": "f181eafd-e587-3759-8591-9e49f61c1ca3",
                                                "text": "1974 [Crane, Russak & Co.], H. Graham Flegg, From Geometry to Topology, 2001, Dover, page v,\nMany university courses in topology plunge immediately into a formalized and entirely abstract presentation of topological concepts."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "dc777f5e-d69e-34d6-b903-d905cb7ea2ca",
                                        "entry_id": "1c745381-18c3-3c7b-9ad8-dc2cddd3487a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any collection τ of subsets of a given set X that contains both the empty set and X, and which is closed under finitary intersections and arbitrary unions."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cc823146-7d17-45f8-8b8e-8023c9daaa05",
                                                "sense_id": "dc777f5e-d69e-34d6-b903-d905cb7ea2ca",
                                                "text": "A set X equipped with a topology #92;tau is called a topological space and denoted (X,#92;tau)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "46524342-d4d0-4a67-9b34-44a9aaf054ec",
                                                "sense_id": "dc777f5e-d69e-34d6-b903-d905cb7ea2ca",
                                                "text": "The subsets of a set X which constitute a topology are called the open sets of X."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a8b0a456-18b2-4c1a-9ad8-e3dbdbabe4ab",
                                                "sense_id": "dc777f5e-d69e-34d6-b903-d905cb7ea2ca",
                                                "text": "It is easy to verify that the topology generated by these basic open sets coincides with the definition of the topology on #92;widehat#123;V#125; above, for the Zariski topology and the sheaf of functions #92;operatorname#123;val#125;(f),f regular."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "21452ea0-1d47-3364-8be5-ce657aa244f2",
                                        "entry_id": "1c745381-18c3-3c7b-9ad8-dc2cddd3487a",
